{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until he was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid. During his captivity *Regency in Antioch* Raymond's cousin Bohemond III of Antioch was captured by the same forces and the Principality of Antioch was left without a leader. The regency was given to Raymond's uncle Amalric I of Jerusalem who was also the King of Jerusalem.\n\nAfter his release from captivity Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a strong supporter of the Knights Templar and was involved in several military campaigns against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. In 1174 Raymond III supported Miles of Plancy as regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem but later switched his allegiance to Raymond of Antioch. *The Battle of Hattin* was a major turning point in the history of the Crusader states and Raymond III played a key role in the events leading up to it. He was one of the leaders of the Crusader forces at the battle but the Crusaders were defeated by Saladin's forces.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy was complex and his actions were often motivated by his own interests and alliances. He was a skilled military leader and politician who played a significant role in the history of the Crusader st